Hello Shiny
library(shiny)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Hello Shiny!"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      sliderInput("num", "Choose a number:", 1, 100, 50)
    ),
    mainPanel(
      textOutput("result")
    )
  )
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  output$result &lt;- renderText({
    paste("You selected:", input$num)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

Walk through the code top to bottom:

ui: what the page looks like. fluidPage is the root; it becomes an HTML page styled with Bootstrap. Resize the window to show it adapting.
server: a function that connects inputs to outputs. session is advanced — we won’t use it.
shinyApp(ui, server) ties them together and starts a web server.

A conductor
server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  samples &lt;- reactive(tibble(
    x1 = rnorm(500, input$mu1, 1),
    x2 = rnorm(500, input$mu2, 1)
  ))

  output$plot &lt;- renderPlot({
    ggplot(samples(), aes(x = x1, y = x2)) +
      geom_point() +
      coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-5, 5), ylim = c(-5, 5))
  })

  output$means &lt;- renderText({
    paste("Sample means:", round(mean(samples()$x1), 2), "and",
          round(mean(samples()$x2), 2))
  })

  output$greeting &lt;- renderText(paste("Hello", input$name))
}

The UI (in app2_samples.R) has two numericInputs (mu1, mu2) and a textInput (name), laid out with fluidRow/column.
coord_cartesian fixes the axes, so the cloud visibly moves rather than the axes rescaling around it.

A counter






Task


An app has two buttons and a line of text:
ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  actionButton("inc", "Increase counter"),
  actionButton("reset", "Reset counter"),
  textOutput("value")
)
Write the server so that the text shows a count that the buttons increase and reset. There’s no input holding the count — where does it live?




server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  counter &lt;- reactiveVal(0)           # a source the server can change

  observeEvent(input$inc, {
    counter(counter() + 1)            # set by calling with a value
  })

  observeEvent(input$reset, {
    counter(0)
  })

  output$value &lt;- renderText({
    paste("Counter is:", counter())   # read by calling it
  })
}


DEMO: run app3_counter.R and click both buttons.

An actionButton’s value is just the number of presses — rarely worth showing. What matters is that a press happened: an event, so pair it with observeEvent. By default observeEvent ignores the starting value 0, so nothing runs on start-up.
The observers change the reactiveVal; the renderText reads it, so it re-runs automatically.
Don’t use an observer to compute a value and stash it in a reactiveVal for others to read — that’s what reactive() is for.

The explorer: code

penguins &lt;- read_csv("data/penguins.csv")   # once, outside the server

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Penguin explorer"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("species", "Choose a species:",
        choices = c("(Choose a species)" = "",
                    "Adelie", "Gentoo", "Chinstrap")),
      sliderInput("mass", "Body mass (g):", min = 2700, max = 6300,
        value = c(2700, 6300), sep = ""),
      checkboxInput("trend", "Show a trend line", value = TRUE)
    ),
    mainPanel(plotOutput("plot"), tableOutput("table"))
  )
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  selected &lt;- reactive({
    penguins |&gt;
      filter(species == input$species,
             body_mass_g &gt;= input$mass[1], body_mass_g &lt;= input$mass[2])
  })

  output$plot &lt;- renderPlot({
    req(input$species)   # do nothing until a species is chosen
    plot &lt;- ggplot(selected(),
                   aes(x = flipper_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
      geom_point() +
      coord_cartesian(xlim = c(170, 235), ylim = c(2700, 6300)) +
      labs(title = paste(input$species, "penguins"),
           x = "Flipper length (mm)", y = "Body mass (g)")
    if (input$trend) plot &lt;- plot + geom_smooth(se = FALSE)
    plot
  })

  output$table &lt;- renderTable({
    req(input$species)
    selected() |&gt;
      summarise(n = n(), mean_mass = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
  })
}


Points to make:

read_csv happens once, when the app starts — not inside the server, where it would re-read the file on every change.
A range slider gives two numbers: input$mass[1] and input$mass[2].
The named empty choice "(Choose a species)" = "" displays a prompt while input$species is "", which req() treats as “not chosen yet”.
coord_cartesian fixes the axes so plots for different species can be compared directly — week 6’s honest-plots principle.

